\begin{quote}
All known ecient algebraic schemes are in the bounded collusion model
and so far, none of them enjoys public traceability. In this paper, we
achieve public traceability without downgrading the eciency of the
proposed sheme.

Public key traitor tracing encryption

A public-key traitor tracing scheme consists of four probabilistic
algorithms Setup, Enc, Dec and Trace.

6

\emph{•} Algorithm Setup is run by a trusted authority. It takes as
inputs a security pa-rameter \emph{λ}, a list of users
(\emph{Ui})\emph{i≤N} and a bound \emph{t} on the size of traitor
coalitions. It computes a public key \emph{pk}, descriptions of the
plaintext and ciphertext domains \emph{P} and \emph{C}, secret keys
(\emph{ski})\emph{i≤N}, and a tracing key \emph{tk} (which may contain
the \emph{ski}\textquotesingle s and additional data). It publishes
\emph{pk, P} and \emph{C}, and sends \emph{ski} to user \emph{Ui} for
all \emph{i ≤N}.\emph{•} Algorithm Enc can be run by any party. It takes
as inputs a public key \emph{pk} and a plaintext message \emph{M ∈P}. It
computes a ciphertext \emph{C ∈C}.

\emph{•} Algorithm Dec can be run by any user. It takes as inputs a
secret key \emph{ski} and a ciphertext message \emph{C ∈C}. It computes
a plaintext \emph{P ∈P}.

\emph{•} Algorithm Trace is explained below. If the input of Trace,
i.e., the tracing key \emph{tk}, is public then we say that the scheme
supports public traceability.

We require that Setup, Enc and Dec run in polynomial time, and that with
over-

whelming probability over the randomness used by the algorithms, we have
\end{quote}

\emph{∀M ∈P, ∀i ≤N} : Dec(\emph{ski,} Enc(\emph{pk, M})) = \emph{M,}

where \emph{pk} and the \emph{ski}\textquotesingle s are sampled from
Setup. We also require the encryption scheme

\begin{quote}
to be IND-CPA.

Algorithm Trace aims at deterring coalitions of malicious users
(traitors) from
\end{quote}

building an unauthorized decryption device. It takes as input \emph{tk}
and has access to a

\begin{quote}
decryption device \emph{D}. Trace aims at disclosing the identity of at
least one user that participated in building \emph{D}.

We consider the minimal black-box access model {[}11{]}. In this model,
the tracing

authority has access to an oracle \emph{OD}that itself internally uses
