The reduction takes an LWE instance and extends it to a related
\emph{k}-LWE instance for which the additional hint vectors
(\emph{\textbf{x}i})\emph{i≤k} are known. The major diculty in this
extension is to restrain the noise increase, as a function of \emph{k}.

The existing approach for this reduction (that we improve below) is the
technique used in the SIS to \emph{k}-SIS reduction from {[}12{]}. In
the latter approach, the hint vectors are chosen independently from a
small discrete Gaussian distribution, and then the is extended to a
taller matrix \emph{A′}under the constraint that the LWE matrix \emph{A
∈}Z\emph{m×n}\\
hint vectors are in the \emph{q}-ary lattice \emph{Λ⊥}(\emph{A′}) =
\emph{\{\textbf{b}} : \emph{\textbf{b}tA′}= \textbf{0} mod \emph{q\}}.
Unfortunately, with this approach, the transformation from an LWE sample
with respect to \emph{A}, to a \emph{k}-LWE sample with respect to
\emph{A′}, involves a multiplication by the cofactor matrix
det(\emph{G}) \emph{· G−}1over Z of a \emph{k × k} full-rank submatrix
\emph{G} of the hint vectors matrix. Although the entries of \emph{G}
are small, the entries of its cofactor matrix are almost as large as det
\emph{G}, which is exponential in \emph{k}. This leads to an exponential
noise blowup, \emph{O}(1) if one wants to rely on the hardness
restraining the applicability range to \emph{k ≤}\\
of LWE with noise rate 1\emph{/α ≤Poly}(\emph{n}) (otherwise, LWE is not
exponentially hard to solve).

To restrain the noise increase for large \emph{k}, we use a matrix pair
(\emph{X, U})
\emph{∈}Z\emph{k×}(\emph{m}+\emph{k})\emph{×}Z(\emph{m}+\emph{k})\emph{×}(\emph{m}+\emph{k})from
Theorem 17. We write \emph{X} = (\textbf{1}\emph{t∥Y} ) and let \emph{V
∈}Z\emph{m×}(\emph{m}+\emph{k})be the matrix made of the bottom \emph{m}
rows of \emph{U} (it is a basis of ker(\emph{X})). At a high level, our
reduction consists in mapping the LWE instance (\emph{A, \textbf{b}}) to
the \emph{k}-LWE in-stance (\emph{VtA, Yt, Vt\textbf{b}}). Note that
\emph{Xt}(\emph{VtA}) = 0 mod \emph{q}, which means that the columns of
\emph{Y} are indeed hint vectors. In the proof of correctness of the
reduction, we will argue that the pair (\emph{VtA, Y} ) is almost
correctly distributed. The distribution imperfection lies in the fact
that some rows of \emph{Y} have non-zero centers, because of Theorem 17.

14

In the proof, we will argue that if an algorithm can eciently solve
\emph{k}-LWE with non-zero-centered hints, it can be used to eciently
solve \emph{k}-LWE with zero-centered hints. Another diculty is that the
noise component of \emph{Vt\textbf{b}} is skewed. We will unskew it by
adding to it some extra Gaussian noise, with a carefully designed
covariance matrix.

Proof. Without loss of generality, we assume that \emph{k} =
\emph{Ω}(\emph{n}) throughout the proof: the result with large values of
\emph{k} implies the result for smaller values of \emph{k}, as we can
always disregard hints. We also assume that \emph{k} =
\emph{O}(\emph{m}), as otherwise the conditions on \emph{σ} and
\emph{σ′}cannot be satised simultaneously.

For a technical reason related to the non-zero centers
\emph{\textbf{δ}i} in the distribution of the hint vectors produced by
Theorem 17, we decompose our reduction from LWE\emph{α,m}+1 to (\emph{k,
S})-LWE into two subreductions. The rst subreduction (outlined above)
reduces LWE\emph{α,m}+1 to (\emph{k, S, C})-LWE\emph{α′,m}+1+\emph{k},
where the \emph{i}th column of \emph{C} is the unit vector
\emph{\textbf{c}i} = (0\emph{m∥\textbf{δ}i})
\emph{∈}R\emph{m}+\emph{k}for \emph{i} = 1\emph{, . . . , k}. The second
subreduction reduces (\emph{k, S, C})-LWE\emph{α′,m}+1+\emph{k} to
(\emph{k, S})-LWE\emph{α′,m}+1+\emph{k}. Both reductions preserve the
LWE dimension \emph{n}. We rst describe and analyze the rst
subreduction, and then explain the second subreduction.
