18

Our technique can be applied to improve the Boneh-Freeman reduction from
SIS to \emph{k}-SIS, from an exponential loss in \emph{k} to a
polynomial loss in \emph{k}. In fact, we map \emph{A} to \emph{A′}=
\emph{VtA} in the same way (except that we do not use and add
\emph{\textbf{u}} on top of the matrix \emph{A}) and use \emph{X} as the
\emph{k}-SIS hints for the new matrix \emph{A′}. Then, whenever the
adversary can output a short vector \emph{\textbf{x}} that is orthogonal
to \emph{A′}, we can also output a short vector \emph{V \textbf{x}}
which is orthogonal to \emph{A}. As \emph{\textbf{x}} is linearly
independent from the rows of \emph{X} and \emph{V} is a basis of the
lattice ker \emph{X}, we obtain that \emph{V \textbf{x} ̸}= \textbf{0}.
Also, the vector \emph{V \textbf{x }}is small, as both \emph{V} and
\emph{\textbf{x}} have small entries. RD may also be used to reduce
\emph{k}-SIS with non-zero-centered hints (with small centers) to
\emph{k}-SIS with zero-centered hints.

4 A lattice-based public-key traitor tracing scheme

In this section, we describe and analyze our basic traitor tracing
scheme. First, we give the underlying multi-user public-key encryption
scheme. We then explain how to implement black-box conrmation tracing.

4.1 A multi-user encryption scheme

The scheme is designed for a given security parameter \emph{n}, a number
of users \emph{N} and a maximum malicious coalition size \emph{t}. It
then involves several parameters \emph{q, m, α, S}. These are set so
that the scheme is correct (decryption works properly on honestly
gen-erated ciphertexts) and secure (semantically secure encryption and
possibility to trace members of malicious coalitions). In particular, we
set \emph{S} = Diag(\emph{σ, . . . , σ, σ′, . . . , σ′})
\emph{∈}R\emph{m×m}where \emph{σ′\textgreater{} σ} and their respective
numbers of iterations are set so that (\emph{t, S})-LWE\emph{α,m}+1 is
hard to solve.
\end{quote}

