5 Projective sampling and public traceability
\end{quote}

We now modify the scheme of Section 4 so that the tracing signals can be
publicly

\begin{quote}
sampled. For this purpose, we introduce the concept of projective
sampling family.

5.1 Projective sampling
\end{quote}

Inspired from the notion of projective hash family {[}23{]}, we propose
the notion of pro-

jective sampling family in which each sampling function is keyed and,
with a projected

key, one can simulate the sampling function in a computationally
indistinguishable

23

\begin{quote}
way. Let \emph{X} be a nite non-empty set. Let \emph{F} =
(F\emph{k})\emph{k∈K} be a collection of sampling functions indexed by
\emph{K}, so that F\emph{k} is a sampling function over \emph{X}, for
every \emph{k ∈K}. We call Sam = (\emph{F, K, X}) a sampling family. We
now introduce the concept of projective sampling.

Denition 26 (Projective Sampling). Let Sam = (\emph{F, K, X}) be a
sampling family. Let \emph{J} be a nite, non-empty set, and let \emph{π}
: \emph{K →J} be a (probabilistic) function. Let also P =
(P\emph{j})\emph{j∈J} be a collection of sampling functions over
\emph{X}, and \emph{D} be a distribution over \emph{K}. Then PSam =
(\emph{F, K, X,} P\emph{, J, π, D}) is called a projective sampling
family if, with overwhelming probability over the choice of \emph{k,
k′←D}, and given the secret key \emph{k} and its projected key
\emph{π}(\emph{k}), 1) the distributions obtained using F\emph{k} and
P\emph{π}(\emph{k}) are computationally indistinguishable, and 2) the
distributions obtained using F\emph{k} and P\emph{π}(\emph{k′}) can be
eciently distinguished.

The rst condition means that for \emph{k ←D}, the value
\emph{π}(\emph{k}) encodes the sampling distribution of F\emph{k}, so
that when \emph{π}(\emph{k}) is made public, the sampled signal
F\emph{k} can be pub-licly simulated by P\emph{π}(\emph{k})\emph{.} The
security requirement is very strong because the adversary is not only
given the projected key, as in projective hashing, but also the secret
key \emph{k}. We require that sampling signals from the secret key and
from its projected key be indistinguishable for the insiders who know
the secret key. This is relevant for traitor tracing, as the traitors
are system insiders and they possess secret data. The second condition
(that we actually do not directly use in our cryptographic application)
al-lows to prevent the trivial solution consisting in setting
P\emph{π}(\emph{k}) as an ecient sampling function that is independent
of \emph{k}: the simulation signal P\emph{π}(\emph{k}) must be specic to
\emph{k}.3

