5.3 Public traceability from projective sampling

In the scheme of Section 4, the tracing key \emph{tk} = (\emph{T,}
(\emph{Ui, \textbf{x}i})\emph{i≤N}) must be kept se-cret, as it would
reveal the secret keys of the users. The tracing signals are samples

from \emph{U}(Span\emph{j≤i}(\emph{\textbf{x}}+ \emph{j})\emph{⊥}) +
\emph{⌊ναq⌉m}+1, which exactly matches F\emph{i,k}. By publishing the
projected key \emph{πi}(\emph{k}), anyone can use the projective
sampling P\emph{i,πi}(\emph{k}): by Theorem 27,
\end{quote}

given (\emph{k, πi}(\emph{k})), F\emph{i,k} and P\emph{i,πi}(\emph{k})
are indistinguishable and they are both indistinguish-

\begin{quote}
able from the original sampling \emph{U}(Im(\emph{A}+))+\emph{⌊ναq⌉m}+1.
We are thus almost done with public traceability.

However, a subtle point is that we have to use all the projective
samplings (P\emph{i,πi}(\emph{k}))

for transforming the secret tracing to the public tracing: all the
projected keys (\emph{\textbf{h}j})\emph{j≤N} should be published.
Because the keys \emph{k} in F\emph{i,k} are not independent, it could
occur

that the adversary exploits a projected key \emph{πi}(\emph{k}) for
distinguishing P\emph{i′,πi′}(\emph{k′}) from the original signals. To
handle this, we prove that, given (\emph{\textbf{x}j})\emph{j≤i} and all
the keys (\emph{\textbf{h}j})\emph{j≤N}, the adversary cannot
distinguish P\emph{i,πi}(\emph{k}) from the original signals. For this
purpose, we

exploit a technique from {[}28{]} to simulate
(\emph{\textbf{h}j})\emph{i\textless j≤N} from the public information.
\end{quote}

