This completes the proof of the lemma. \emph{⊔}

We note that, in Game1, the \emph{\textbf{h}j}\textquotesingle s can be
sampled publicly from the available data. Therefore, from Theorem 27,
under the (\emph{i, S})-LWE\emph{α,m} hardness assumptions, the
ad-vantage \emph{ε}1 is negligible. \emph{⊔}Semantic security of the
updated scheme. We modify the public information of the scheme of
Section 4, so that we can use the set of projective sampling families
de-scribed above. For this aim, we simply add the projected key
(\emph{H,} (\emph{\textbf{h}i})\emph{i≤N}) to the public key. The scheme
becomes publicly traceable because the tracing signals can be sampled
from the projected keys, as explained above. Finally, as the public key
has been modied, we should prove that the knowledge of these projected
keys provides no signicant advantage for an adversary towards breaking
the semantic security of the encryption scheme. Fortunately, the
semantic security directly follows from Theo-rem 28, for the particular
case of \emph{i} = 0.

